WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.669
This is San Marcos, Texas.

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.903
Over a century ago,

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.405
the United States
Fish Commission

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.708
dug a well plunging 188
feet into the earth

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:10.677
right at the heart
of the city.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.179
And when they did, water
rushed to the surface

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:15.281
at thousands of 
gallons a minute,

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.650
flushing out dozens of
undiscovered creatures

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.486
from the depths below.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:21.821
These animals had never
seen the surface.

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:22.789
Nor would they,

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.225
because all of 
them were blind.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.727
Perhaps the strangest-looking
animal in this catch

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:30.397
was a species dubbed
Eurycea rathbuni,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.299
now known as the Texas
blind salamander.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.269
Upon discovery, herpetologist
Leonhard Stejneger

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.004
described them as such:

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:57.891
Narrator: These unusual 
conditions are contained

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:00.660
to a specific 26-mile
stretch of caves

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.195
in the Edwards Aquifer,

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:04.664
an extensive underground
layer of rocks.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.366
It's the only 
place in the world

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.536
this species of salamander
has ever been recorded.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.305
So, how did these caves create
the "profound differences"

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.607
in these salamanders'
appearance?

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:18.011
In other words, how did they
end up looking so weird?

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:18.978
To answer this question,

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.914
we have to go back in time.

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:22.015
Way back.

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:24.050
Tom Devitt: Take the dates
with a grain of salt,

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.287
but we think that 
this group split off

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.524
from its most recent common
ancestor probably around

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:33.860
somewhere between 10 and
15 million years ago.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:35.895
Narrator: And geologists 
estimate that the aquifer

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:39.065
had been hundreds of millions
of years in the making.

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:42.802
During the Jurassic period,
some 200 million years ago,

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:46.172
the area was occasionally 
covered in shallow oceans,

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.708
but when the area was 
dry and exposed to air,

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.278
the rocks were eroded by 
weather to make porous caverns.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.747
Eventually, a shallow basin
of water and sediment

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.216
covered the land.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.184
Then that basin dried up.

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.653
But rainfall continued 
to eat away at the earth

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:04.390
to create the deep fissures and
caverns inside the aquifer.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.892
Scientists like Devitt think

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:07.961
that as the climate
became more arid,

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.463
some salamanders crawled
beneath the surface,

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:13.299
seeking
refuge in the cool and stable environment

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.101
of the Edwards Aquifer.

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:17.237
And over the course 
of millions of years,

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.974
these salamanders adapted to 
the aquatic subterranean caves

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:22.909
by evolving 
certain features,

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.512
like blood-red gills 
outside of their body,

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:28.481
while losing others,
like their eyes.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:29.849
Living in complete darkness

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:32.352
alongside prey that
was also blind,

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.721
the salamanders simply
didn't need to see,

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:37.790
and energy used for sight
would only be wasted.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.127
Today, only two small black
dots just under the skin

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:43.563
remain as evidence
of eyes past.

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:47.433
Those dots likely only detect
if it's light or dark out.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.903
This use-it-or-lose-it
type of evolution

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.705
also applies to their
silvery white skin.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:55.208
Skin pigmentation 
usually protects animals

00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:57.010
from the sun's UV light

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:59.812
and serves as camouflage
from predators,

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:03.016
but both of those are nonissues
for the blind salamanders,

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:06.219
who don't have to worry 
about the sun or predators.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:08.421
These small, 
spindly salamanders

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.291
are at the top of the food
chain in this ecosystem,

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:14.193
feeding on various 
crustaceans, like shrimp.

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.563
And even though they can 
go months without eating,

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:18.932
when a potential 
snack does swim by,

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:21.801
several features help 
them capture their prey.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.403
One is a special adaptation

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.239
similar to the lateral
line found in fish,

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.775
which detects vibrations
in the water.

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.944
The salamanders' 
sensory cells respond

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:33.379
to changes in water 
movement and pressure,

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:34.981
letting them know
what direction

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:37.984
and how fast their
meal is moving.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.520
Another helpful feature
is their large head,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.022
which fits 10% to
15% more teeth

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.058
than surface salamanders.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:47.493
More teeth means
a deadlier bite,

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:49.696
which is crucial if
you come by prey

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:51.464
only a few times a year.

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:53.299
Danté Fenolio: All of those
things are characteristics

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.300
that help you survive

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:57.036
in a very, very 
challenging environment,

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:59.839
so that's why they look
the way that they do.

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.608
Narrator: Fenolio is
one of the lucky few

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:04.477
who've seen these elusive
salamanders up close.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.713
Last year, his team at
the San Antonio Zoo

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:10.483
bred Texas blind salamanders
for the first time in a lab,

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.286
and they've repeated their
success four more times.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:16.255
They're aiming to protect 
the species from extinction,

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:20.259
since the salamanders have 
been endangered since 1967.

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:23.596
Two main things threaten the
Texas salamander's survival.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.733
The first is contamination
from wastewater runoff.

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:28.067
This is a big issue,

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:31.404
because the Edwards Aquifer 
is a so-called karst aquifer,

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:34.307
which means it's made of 
soluble rock like limestone.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.708
Any pollution on the surface

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:39.112
can rapidly seep into and
contaminate groundwater.

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:42.015
Wastewater typically 
holds tons of nutrients,

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:44.183
which fuels the 
growth of algae.

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:45.918
When the algae decompose,

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.755
this process sucks 
oxygen from the water,

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:52.258
leaving less oxygen for 
our sensitive salamanders.

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:54.460
The second threat is
a loss of habitat.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.096
This happens when rainfall 
cannot adequately replenish

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:58.965
the water that people take,

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:00.667
and this threat is real.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.535
The aquifer serves
several cities,

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.070
including San Antonio,

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.572
one of the fastest-growing
cities in the nation

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.241
and the largest 
city in the country

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:11.444
that relies entirely on 
an aquifer for its water.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:13.613
So while being able to
breed the salamanders

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:17.050
is a good first step towards 
one day building up colonies,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.985
the animal may be
confined to labs

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.187
if they have no 
home to return to.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.089
Devitt: You know, in 
areas where there's been

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:25.425
hundreds of feet of 
groundwater depletion,

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:28.828
we've almost certainly 
already lost species to that,

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.263
but we just don't
know about it,

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:32.999
because we're still 
describing new species

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.368
from the aquifer
all the time.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:37.003
Narrator: Devitt's team
is using new tools

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.706
to suss out blind 
salamander populations,

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:42.175
such as a method that 
analyzes water samples

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.577
for traces of the
animal's DNA.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:45.912
He and his collaborator

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.448
recently discovered four
new related species

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:50.016
in the aquifer.

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:51.150
For now, scientists say

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.852
that these salamanders
can hang in there,

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:56.422
as long as the only home they've
ever known stays constant,

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:58.057
but that's an 
increasing challenge

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:00.993
as more people 
move to the area.

